    "description": "For a house with a natural gas line and an existing flue, this is the 50-gallon tank to start with. The PROG50-40N RH62 is Rheem's Professional Classic atmospheric model with a 40,000 BTU burner, and Rheem's spec table lists 40.4 gallons per hour of recovery at a 90-degree rise and an 83-gallon first-hour rating. That is more than either electric tank above, which is the main reason gas remains the better 50-gallon tank where gas is available: the burner reheats water roughly twice as fast as a pair of electric elements. Atmospheric venting means no blower, no power cord and nothing to fail in an outage; the hot exhaust rises up the existing chimney or B-vent on its own. The cost of that simplicity is efficiency, with a UEF in the low 0.60s, so expect to spend somewhat more on gas than the condensing or power-vent alternatives. Rheem's product page lists a six-year tank and six-year parts warranty and gives the dimensions as about 59 inches tall and 21.5 inches wide. It is the gas equivalent of the Rheem electric above: a contractor-grade tank without extras."
