          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best 50 Gallon Water Heater",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-50-gallon-water-heater/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How many people does a 50 gallon water heater serve?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A 50-gallon tank usually serves three to five people. Three people with staggered showers are comfortable with any 50-gallon tank. Four people with a busy morning need a first-hour rating in the 70s or higher, which means gas or a 5,500W electric. Five people should look at a high-input power vent gas tank, which can reach well over 100 gallons in the first hour."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a 50 gallon gas or electric water heater better?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Gas recovers about twice as fast and costs less to run in most regions, so a 50-gallon gas tank delivers more hot water in the first hour. Electric is simpler to install, needs no flue and can go in a closet. In practice, the fuel your house already has usually decides it, because changing fuel adds a gas line or a new circuit to the cost."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is a good first-hour rating for a 50 gallon water heater?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Standard 4,500W electric tanks list around 60 to 63 gallons, 5,500W electric tanks around 70 to 76, atmospheric gas tanks around 80 to 85, and high-input power vent tanks 100 gallons or more. Match the rating to your household's worst hour with about a 10 percent margin, and you will rarely run out."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How tall is a 50 gallon water heater?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It depends on the shape. Short and medium electric tanks are about 48 to 53 inches tall, standard tall tanks about 58 to 62 inches, and hybrid heat pump tanks close to 79 inches. Add a few inches for the water connections and relief valve, and for gas tanks allow extra height for the vent connector above the draft hood."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does a 50 gallon water heater last?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most tank water heaters last eight to twelve years. The warranty length is a rough guide, but maintenance matters more: flushing sediment once a year and replacing the anode rod when it is worn can add years. Hard water shortens tank life, and a tank installed without an expansion tank on a closed system may fail sooner from pressure cycling."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What size breaker does a 50 gallon electric water heater need?",
