        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A 50 gallon water heater is the right tank for most homes of three to five people, but the 50 on the label tells you only half of what you need to know. The tanks on this page all hold the same water and deliver anywhere from 62 to 119 gallons in their first hour, and the difference between those numbers is the difference between a family that never thinks about hot water and one that schedules showers. Pick the first-hour rating for your worst hour, then the fuel your house already has, then the shape that fits.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="who-fits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Who a 50-Gallon Water Heater Actually Fits
          </h2>

          <p>
            A 50 gallon water heater is the standard tank for a household of three to five people, and it is the most commonly installed size in American homes for a reason: it covers a normal morning without the cost, weight and footprint of a 75-gallon tank. But the number on the label is the wrong place to start. Two 50-gallon tanks on this page deliver 62 and 119 gallons of hot water in their first hour. Same capacity, nearly double the useful output. Who a 50-gallon tank fits depends far more on how fast it reheats than on how much it holds.
          </p>

          <p>
            The simplest way to size is to count your worst hour, not your average day. In most homes that is the morning: two or three showers back to back, a sink running, perhaps a dishwasher or a load of laundry on warm. A modern low-flow showerhead uses about 2 gallons a minute, and roughly 70 percent of that is hot water at typical settings. A ten-minute shower therefore takes about 14 gallons of tank water. Three of those plus 6 gallons at the sinks and a 6-gallon dishwasher cycle is roughly 54 gallons in a single hour. Four showers takes it to about 68.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is the household a 50-gallon tank is built for. A family of three with staggered showers will rarely notice a limit. A family of four with an hour-long morning rush is right at the edge of a basic 4,500W electric tank and comfortably inside a gas tank or a 5,500W electric. A family of five, or anyone with a 60-gallon soaking tub, needs one of the high-recovery models below, or a step up in size.
          </p>

          <p>
