        <div id="first-hour" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. First-Hour Rating vs Capacity: The Number That Decides It
          </h2>

          <p>
            The first-hour rating, or FHR, is the number of gallons of hot water a tank can deliver in one hour starting with a full tank of hot water. It is printed on the yellow EnergyGuide label and on every spec sheet, and it is the closest thing the industry has to a measure of what you will actually experience. It combines two things: the usable hot water already in the tank, and how much new water the heat source can bring up to temperature within that hour.
          </p>

          <p>
            Capacity sets the first part, and every tank on this page has the same nominal 50 gallons. The heat source sets the second part, and that is where they split apart. A pair of 4,500W electric elements can only run one at a time in a standard tank, so recovery is slow, around 20 gallons an hour at a 90-degree rise. A 40,000 BTU gas burner manages around 40 gallons an hour. A 62,000 BTU power vent burner manages far more. The result is the spread you see in the table below.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
