                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">62 gallons</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">GE GE50S10BAM</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 x 5,500W electric</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">76 gallons</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Rheem PROG50-40N RH62</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">40,000 BTU gas</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">83 gallons</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">A.O. Smith GPVX-50L</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">62,000 BTU gas, power vent</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">119 gallons</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Ratings are as listed by the manufacturer or on the product listing. We have left out figures we could not confirm for this exact model, including the Bradford White and the Rheem ProTerra, rather than guess.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical rule is simple. Match the first-hour rating to your worst hour from the section above, with a margin of about 10 percent. If your peak is 60 gallons, a 62-gallon electric tank will work but will leave the last shower lukewarm on bad days, and a 76 or 83-gallon tank will not. If your peak is 80 gallons, only the gas tanks will keep up, and if it is close to 100, you want the power vent or a larger tank.
          </p>

          <p>
            One caution on heat pump water heaters: their first-hour rating depends on the mode. In pure heat pump mode the compressor is slow, so recovery drops well below a standard electric tank. In hybrid or high-demand mode the backup elements switch on and the figure rises close to a standard tank. If you buy a hybrid for a family of four or five, plan on running it in hybrid mode at least during the morning rush, or you will notice the slower recovery.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Buy the first-hour rating, not the gallons.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A 50-gallon tank with a 119-gallon first hour serves more people than a 75-gallon electric tank with a slow recovery. Size to your worst hour, then pick the fuel and the footprint.</p>
