        <div id="fuel" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Gas vs Electric vs Hybrid at 50 Gallons
          </h2>

          <p>
            At 50 gallons the choice of fuel is usually made for you by what the house already has. Replacing a gas tank with a gas tank or an electric with an electric is the cheapest swap, because the venting, gas line or 240V circuit is already in place. Changing fuel is possible, but it adds a gas line or a new circuit to the bill, and that can cost more than the water heater. Still, it helps to know what each option gives you at this size.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Gas: faster recovery, cheaper fuel in most regions</h3>

          <p>
            A 50 gallon gas water heater recovers roughly twice as fast as a standard electric tank, which is why gas tanks post first-hour ratings in the 80s and above. In most of the country, natural gas is also cheaper per unit of heat delivered than electricity, even after the efficiency penalty of an atmospheric tank. The drawbacks are the flue, which must be inspected and kept clear, and combustion: a gas tank needs combustion air and a properly drafting vent, and it should be near a working carbon monoxide alarm. Our <a href="/best-gas-water-heater/">best gas water heaters</a> guide covers every gas size and venting type.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Electric: simplest install, slowest recovery</h3>

          <p>
            A 50 gallon electric water heater needs only a 240V circuit and water lines. There is no flue, no combustion and nothing to light, so it can sit in a closet, an attic or a finished room. Standard electric tanks are rated around 0.92 to 0.93 UEF, meaning very little heat is lost to venting, but electricity costs more per unit of heat in most regions, and two-element tanks recover slowly. Our <a href="/best-electric-water-heater/">best electric water heaters</a> guide ranks electric tanks of every size.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hybrid heat pump: the cheapest to run, the hardest to fit</h3>

          <p>
            A hybrid tank is an electric tank with a heat pump on top. It needs the same 240V circuit as a standard electric, but it uses roughly a quarter of the electricity. The price is upfront cost, height, a condensate drain and a cooler room. Federal tax credits and utility rebates often close much of the price gap; check what your utility offers before you dismiss it.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the house has gas and a flue, a gas 50 is the default. If it is all electric and the tank sits in a garage or a large basement, the hybrid is the one to look at hardest. If it is all electric and the tank lives in a closet, a standard electric tank with good recovery is usually the only one that fits.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="uef" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. UEF and What a Year of Hot Water Costs
          </h2>

          <p>
            The Uniform Energy Factor, or UEF, is the efficiency rating the Department of Energy requires on every residential water heater. A higher number means less energy per gallon of hot water. It is measured under standard test conditions, so it is best used to compare tanks against each other rather than to predict your exact bill. Still, a rough cost comparison shows why fuel and type matter far more than brand.
          </p>

          <p>
            Here is a simple estimate for a household that uses 64 gallons of hot water a day, heated by 67 degrees. Heating that water takes about 35,800 BTU a day, the equivalent of about 10.5 kWh of heat. Divide by the UEF to get the energy the heater consumes, then multiply by a year and by your local energy price. We have used $0.17 per kWh for electricity and $1.50 per therm for natural gas, close to recent US residential averages; your rates may be very different, so substitute your own.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Tank type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">UEF</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Energy per year</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Estimated cost per year</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Standard electric (Rheem, GE)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0.93</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 4,100 kWh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $700</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Atmospheric gas (Rheem)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0.62</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 210 therms</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $315</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Power vent gas (A.O. Smith)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0.73</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 180 therms</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $270</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Hybrid heat pump (Rheem ProTerra)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3.88</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 990 kWh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $170</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            These are our arithmetic from the rated UEF and the assumptions stated above, not measured bills. Real-world hybrid savings are usually lower than the rated figure suggests, because hybrids switch on their elements in heavy demand and work less efficiently in cold rooms. Even so, the gap between a standard electric tank and a hybrid is large enough that a hybrid generally pays back its higher price within a few years in an electric home, and faster where rebates apply.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two conclusions follow. First, an electric home pays roughly double what a gas home pays for the same hot water with a standard tank, which is why the hybrid matters so much more on the electric side. Second, between two tanks of the same type, UEF differences are small: a 0.93 electric and a 0.92 electric cost almost exactly the same to run. Do not pay a premium for a hundredth of a point. Pay for first-hour rating, warranty and fit instead.
          </p>
        </div>

