          <p>
            Sizes come from manufacturer and retailer listings and vary slightly between sources; the water connections and relief valve add height above the tank itself. Gas tanks also need vent height above the draft hood, so leave room for the flue connector to rise before it turns.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tall tanks are narrower, which is useful between a furnace and a wall or in a tight corner of a basement. Short and medium tanks are wider and suit closets with a shelf, spaces under stairs, and basements with low beams. The hybrid is in a class of its own: at nearly 79 inches tall it needs about 7 feet of clear height once you allow for the connections, and the heat pump needs air. Manufacturers typically call for several hundred cubic feet of surrounding space, or ducting or a louvered door, so check the installation manual for the exact figure before you plan on a closet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clearance matters as much as footprint. You need room in front of the tank to reach the thermostat, gas control or element covers and the drain valve. Many codes call for a drain pan under any tank installed where a leak would damage the building, and a thermal expansion tank on a closed system, which needs its own space on the cold line. Our guide to the <a href="/water-heater-expansion-tank/">water heater expansion tank</a> explains when one is required. In a garage, many local codes still require a gas tank to sit on a platform 18 inches above the floor, which can push a tall tank past the ceiling of a low garage. Ask your local building department; the rule depends on which code edition your town has adopted.
