        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Warranty Years: What Six, Eight and Ten Actually Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            Tank water heaters are often sold by warranty length: six, eight, ten or twelve years. The longer warranty does not always mean a different tank. In many product lines, the more expensive models add a second or larger anode rod, thicker insulation or a better drain valve, and the manufacturer backs that with a longer warranty. In some cases the tank is essentially the same and the extra years are what you pay for.
          </p>

          <p>
            The warranty usually covers the tank against leaks for the full term, and parts such as thermostats, elements and gas controls for a shorter or matching term. It rarely covers labor after the first year, and replacing a leaking tank under warranty normally means you get a new tank but pay a plumber to install it. Read what is covered before you pay extra for years.
          </p>

          <p>
            On this page, the Rheem Professional Classic tanks and the A.O. Smith power vent carry six-year limited warranties on the manufacturers' listings, and the Rheem ProTerra carries ten. Bradford White sells its standard residential tanks with a six-year limited warranty through its distributors. Rheem's Performance Platinum line, the 50-gallon tank many readers ask about, is sold with longer warranties of up to twelve years, but it is a Home Depot line that Amazon does not stock, which is why the Professional Classic appears here instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            The single biggest factor in how long any tank lasts is the anode rod, the sacrificial metal rod that corrodes so the steel tank does not. Most tanks last eight to twelve years; checking the anode every three to four years and replacing it when it is worn can add years beyond that. Our guide to <a href="/water-heater-anode-rod-replacement/">water heater anode rod replacement</a> covers how to check yours, and <a href="/how-long-do-water-heaters-last/">how long water heaters last</a> covers what shortens their life. Flushing sediment once a year matters too, especially in hard-water areas.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="too-small" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. When 50 Gallons Is Too Small, and When It Is Too Big
          </h2>

          <p>
            A 50-gallon tank is too small when your peak hour regularly runs past its first-hour rating. The signs are familiar: the last person in the morning gets a lukewarm shower, the tub never fills hot, or the dishwasher and a shower cannot run together. Before you buy a bigger tank, check whether the problem is capacity at all. A failed lower element on an electric tank, a thick layer of sediment, or a broken dip tube can all make a healthy-sized tank feel small, and they cost far less to fix.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the size really is the problem, you have three choices. First, keep 50 gallons and buy more recovery: a 5,500W electric, a gas tank in place of a basic electric if you have gas, or a high-input power vent gas tank. The A.O. Smith on this page delivers more in its first hour than many 75-gallon tanks. Second, step up to a 65 or 75-gallon tank, which our <a href="/best-gas-water-heater/">gas water heater guide</a> covers, accepting a larger footprint and more standby loss. Third, consider whether a tankless unit fits your house better; our guide to the <a href="/best-tankless-water-heater/">best tankless water heaters</a> covers the sizing math.
          </p>

          <p>
            A 50-gallon tank is too big when two people or fewer live in the house, or when the household has shrunk since the tank went in. You pay to keep 50 gallons hot around the clock, and you lose energy every hour through the tank walls. Going down to 40 gallons saves a little on running cost and a little on the purchase price, but mainly it saves space. If the existing tank is a 50 and it suits you, there is no need to downsize just because it is technically more than you need.
          </p>

          <p>
            Remember too that turning the thermostat up stretches a tank further. Raising the setting from 120 to 130 degrees means you mix more cold water at the tap, so the same tank serves more showers. It also raises the scald risk and standby loss, so if you go higher than 120, fit a thermostatic mixing valve on the outlet to keep the water at the taps safe.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="install" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Replacing Like for Like: Venting, Wiring and Cost
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most 50-gallon purchases replace a failing 50-gallon tank, and the cheapest job keeps everything the same: fuel, vent type, connection positions and rough height. That lets a plumber reuse the vent connector, gas flex line or electrical whip and the water connections with small changes. Changing type adds work, and the extra work is what drives the price up.
          </p>

          <p>
            For electric tanks, check the circuit. Both the 4,500W and 5,500W tanks here run on a 240V, 30-amp double-pole breaker with 10-gauge copper wire. A 5,500W tank draws about 23 amps, which is within that circuit, but if the existing tank was a smaller 3,500W unit on lighter wire, the wiring may need an upgrade. A hybrid uses the same 240V circuit as a standard electric tank, plus a condensate drain line or pump.
          </p>

          <p>
            For gas, match the venting. An atmospheric tank like the Rheem needs a chimney or B-vent with proper draft and the right connector size. A power vent tank like the A.O. Smith needs a 120V outlet and a PVC vent run within the manufacturer's maximum length, which is often the easiest solution when the old chimney is being removed or lined. The gas line must also be sized for the burner; a 62,000 BTU burner needs more gas than a 40,000 BTU one, so confirm the line can carry it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Installation cost depends on the region and the job. A like-for-like swap is normally the cheapest; adding venting, running a new circuit or moving the tank costs more. Our guide to <a href="/water-heater-replacement-cost/">water heater replacement cost</a> breaks down the typical labor and parts. Many manufacturers also require installation by a licensed professional for full warranty coverage, so read the warranty terms before deciding to do it yourself. If you are draining the old tank yourself before the plumber arrives, our guide on <a href="/how-to-drain-a-water-heater/">how to drain a water heater</a> walks through it safely.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="brands" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Rheem, A.O. Smith, Bradford White and State: How the Brands Split
          </h2>

          <p>
            Four names make most of the tank water heaters sold in the US, and they are less different than their marketing suggests. Rheem (with its Ruud sister brand) and A.O. Smith (which also owns State, Reliance and American) between them build a large share of residential tanks, and Bradford White is the main independent. At 50 gallons, the tanks from these makers use similar steel, glass lining, anodes and foam insulation; what changes is where they are sold, how they are supported, and how the model ranges are priced.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rheem sells its Performance range through Home Depot and its Professional Classic range through plumbing wholesalers, and the two overlap heavily in design. A.O. Smith sells mainly through wholesalers and Lowe's, with State under its own name through contractors. Bradford White sells only through professional channels, which is why its tanks show up on Amazon from third-party sellers rather than from the brand. Our <a href="/water-heater-brands/">water heater brands</a> guide covers the full family tree.
          </p>

          <p>
            State tanks are worth a mention because many readers ask about them. State is an A.O. Smith brand sold almost entirely through contractors, and we could not find a current State 50-gallon tank listed on Amazon US, which is why none appears in our picks. If your plumber quotes a State tank, treat it as the A.O. Smith equivalent; the important comparison is first-hour rating, venting and warranty, not the name on the jacket.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical brand advice is this: buy the tank whose parts your plumber stocks. A gas control valve or thermostat that is on the van can be swapped the same day. One that has to be ordered can leave you without hot water for a week. Ask your installer which brand they service most, and if it is one of the four above, that is a better reason to choose it than any feature list.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on gallons instead of first-hour rating</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Two 50-gallon tanks can differ by more than 50 gallons of hot water in the first hour. If the old tank ran out, a new tank with the same heat source will run out too. Compare first-hour ratings on the EnergyGuide label against your worst hour, and buy recovery, not capacity.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the height of the replacement</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Tank heights for the same 50 gallons range from about 48 inches to almost 79. A tall tank will not fit under a closet shelf, and a hybrid will not fit under most basement ductwork. Measure the space, the door and the clearance above the old tank before you order anything that ships freight.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Switching from atmospheric to power vent without a plan</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A power vent tank needs a 120V outlet, a PVC vent route within the maximum length and a wall to exit through. It also stops heating in a power cut. These are often good trade-offs, but they need planning, and a plumber should confirm the vent run before the old tank comes out.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Upsizing the elements without checking the circuit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 5,500W electric tank draws about 23 amps at 240V. That is fine on a proper 30-amp circuit with 10-gauge copper, but some older tanks were installed on lighter wire. Check the breaker and wire size, or have an electrician check them, before swapping a 3,500W or 4,500W tank for a 5,500W one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the expansion tank and drain pan</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many local codes require a thermal expansion tank on homes with a check valve or pressure-reducing valve, and a drain pan wherever a leak would damage the building. Leaving them out can fail an inspection and shorten the tank's life through repeated pressure spikes. Budget for both when you price the job.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many people does a 50 gallon water heater serve?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 50-gallon tank usually serves three to five people. Three people with staggered showers are comfortable with any 50-gallon tank. Four people with a busy morning need a first-hour rating in the 70s or higher, which means gas or a 5,500W electric. Five people should look at a high-input power vent gas tank, which can reach well over 100 gallons in the first hour.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 50 gallon gas or electric water heater better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Gas recovers about twice as fast and costs less to run in most regions, so a 50-gallon gas tank delivers more hot water in the first hour. Electric is simpler to install, needs no flue and can go in a closet. In practice, the fuel your house already has usually decides it, because changing fuel adds a gas line or a new circuit to the cost.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a good first-hour rating for a 50 gallon water heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Standard 4,500W electric tanks list around 60 to 63 gallons, 5,500W electric tanks around 70 to 76, atmospheric gas tanks around 80 to 85, and high-input power vent tanks 100 gallons or more. Match the rating to your household's worst hour with about a 10 percent margin, and you will rarely run out.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How tall is a 50 gallon water heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the shape. Short and medium electric tanks are about 48 to 53 inches tall, standard tall tanks about 58 to 62 inches, and hybrid heat pump tanks close to 79 inches. Add a few inches for the water connections and relief valve, and for gas tanks allow extra height for the vent connector above the draft hood.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a 50 gallon water heater last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most tank water heaters last eight to twelve years. The warranty length is a rough guide, but maintenance matters more: flushing sediment once a year and replacing the anode rod when it is worn can add years. Hard water shortens tank life, and a tank installed without an expansion tank on a closed system may fail sooner from pressure cycling.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size breaker does a 50 gallon electric water heater need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A typical 50-gallon electric tank with 4,500W or 5,500W elements runs on a 240V, 30-amp double-pole breaker with 10-gauge copper wire. A hybrid heat pump tank usually needs the same circuit, though some models specify a different breaker, so check the installation manual. Smaller 3,500W tanks are sometimes wired on lighter circuits that will not suit a 5,500W replacement.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 50 gallon hybrid water heater worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In an all-electric home with enough space, usually yes. Rated at around 3.9 UEF against 0.93 for a standard electric tank, a hybrid can cut the electric water-heating bill by roughly three quarters, and rebates and tax credits often cover much of the price difference. It is not worth it where it will not fit, in a small closed closet, or in a home already on cheap natural gas.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

